sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control--claude-opus-5-5--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Equivalence test of lattice regularity across silica, SrSO4 and organic shells" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control> ;
    dct:description "Test the mineral-independence claim (thompson1917-ch09-15) directly: compare mesh-regularity metrics from approach 1 (vertex-angle SD, mu2, hexagon fraction at matched polydispersity) between silica lattice shells (Spumellaria), strontium sulphate lattice shells (Acantharia with latticed shells) and the organic lattice of Clathrulina. Use two one-sided tests (TOST) with an equivalence margin fixed in advance from a pilot on synthetic lattices (the margin is set to half the difference in each metric between the matched foam reference and the hard-core Voronoi null of approach 1, so 'equivalent' means closer than the physics-versus-packing distinction can resolve), with mixed models by taxon. Equivalence supports mineral-independent moulding; a mineral effect beyond the margin refutes it. The underlying vesicle pattern is not observable in fixed shells, so this tests mineral-independence of the final geometry, not 'given the same vesicle pattern'; that limitation is stated." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "TOST result per metric across the three mineral groups, with at least 10 shells each." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Fewer than 10 usable Clathrulina or latticed acantharian images, leaving the comparison underpowered." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Scout images for each mineral group, with at least 10 shells per group; Clathrulina SEM is the likely bottleneck. 2. Pre-register metrics, margin and the mixed-model TOST. 3. Run approach 1's pipeline on all shells. 4. Report equivalence or difference per metric." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): long-shot" ;
    skos:note "Missing: Open micrographs of Acantharia lattice shells and Clathrulina in sufficient number; no culture or microscope to generate them." ;
    skos:note "Tool: cellpose" ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:note "Tool: r-lme4" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-14> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-15> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.mikrotax.org/radiolaria/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-matsci-062910-095803> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "How much of biomineral form is physical, and how much is cell-controlled?" .

  <https://www.mikrotax.org/radiolaria/> dct:title "mikrotax Radiolaria" ;
    dct:description "Silica radiolarian images." .
  <https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-matsci-062910-095803> dct:title "Crystallization Pathways in Biomineralization" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Weiner and Addadi 2011, Crystallization pathways in biomineralization" .
}

